## 1. What this covers

This license covers every version of the Arcaeon app, the arcaeon package, the seal and the door released from now on. Copies of earlier versions that were released under the MIT license keep that license for those copies; nothing here takes it back. Using the Arcaeon witness, from any version of anything, is covered by the Witness Terms of Service, which apply to everyone who connects to it.

## 2. Free, for everyone

You may read the code. You may install and use Arcaeon for yourself, on machines you control, for your own records, your own work and your own agents. You may use the open record formats and the open checker to check any Arcaeon record or receipt, yours or anyone else's, without asking us.

## 3. Needs a paid license from us

You need a written license from Arcaeon to:

a. use the code, or a modified copy of it, in a product or service that you sell, or that a company uses to make money;

b. run a witness, or any service that stamps or vouches for records the way the Arcaeon witness does, built on our code or our record formats;

c. offer the software to other people as a hosted service.

Write to legal@arcaeon.io. We would rather say yes on fair terms than say no.

## 4. Never allowed

Using our code or formats to run a witness that routes around ours without a license; removing or changing our copyright and license notices; saying a record was witnessed by Arcaeon when it was not.

## 5. Why the formats are open

The record formats and the checker are published so anyone can verify a record without having to trust us. Reading them and writing tools that check records is free. Using them to run a competing witness is item 3b. If you like everything else about Arcaeon, we ask that you consider our witness: it is how the project pays for itself and stays free for personal use.

## 6. Your records are yours

Your records are yours; the software that keeps them is ours. Nothing in this license gives us any right to your records.

## 7. Unchanged from the current license

No warranty: a sealed record proves the record has not changed since it was written, not that what was written is true. Limitation of liability, governing law (California, San Bernardino County courts), and notices to legal@arcaeon.io stay as they are today, along with the trademark notice.

They read, word for word:

